+#![allow(dead_code)]
+// the stub keeps the state the implementation will read; the reference
+// solution drops the allowance, because by then every field is read.
+
+//! The state at one index, kept so that the log before it can be thrown away.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.4-snapshots` is the step where the log stops being able to grow for ever. A node that
+//! has applied its state up to index 100 does not need entries 1..100 any more: it needs the *state*
+//! those entries produced. A snapshot is that state, plus the two numbers that say where it came
+//! from — the index and the term of the last entry it covers.
+//!
+//! Those two numbers are not decoration. A snapshot is only usable if the node that installs it can
+//! answer two questions about it: *which* entry it replaces (the index) and *which term* that entry
+//! belonged to (the term). The term is what makes the check possible: a follower that already has
+//! an entry at that index can compare the two, and if the terms differ the snapshot is from a
+//! different history and has to be refused. That is the same "log matching" idea `5.3` used for
+//! entries, applied to a whole prefix.
+//!
+//! The payload is opaque here, and deliberately so: what a snapshot *contains* is the state
+//! machine's business — `5.10` is the step that decides what a state is and that applying it is
+//! deterministic. This step is about *where* the snapshot is taken, *when* the log may be compacted
+//! and *how* a node that is too far behind catches up.
+
+use crate::error::Result;
+use crate::raft::{Entry, Index, Term};
+
+/// A state, and the entry it came from.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Snapshot {
+    /// The index of the last entry this snapshot covers.
+    pub last_included_index: Index,
+    /// The term of that entry.
+    pub last_included_term: Term,
+    /// The state itself — opaque bytes as far as this step is concerned.
+    pub data: Vec<u8>,
+}
