+# Snapshots: the state at an index
+
+A log that only grows is a log that eventually cannot be replicated, restored or shipped. This page
+is the project's answer, and it is the same answer every replicated log has found: **keep the state
+you have already applied, and throw away the entries that produced it.**
+
+## What a snapshot is
+
+A snapshot is three things:
+
+- the **state** itself — the bytes the state machine reached;
+- the **index** of the last entry it covers;
+- the **term** of that entry.
+
+The first is the payload and this step does not look inside it: what a state *is* is
+[the state machine's business](#what-the-state-is-not), and `5.10` is the step that decides what a
+state is and that applying it is deterministic.
+
+The other two are what make a snapshot usable. A node that installs a snapshot has to answer two
+questions about it: which entry does it replace, and which term did that entry belong to? The term
+is the interesting one, because it is what lets a node check the snapshot against what it already
+has: if the follower holds an entry at that index and the terms differ, the snapshot is from a
+different history and has to be refused. That is the same *log matching* idea the entries use,
+applied to a whole prefix.
+
+## When the log is compacted
+
+A snapshot is taken of the **committed** state and of nothing else — a snapshot of an entry that is
+not committed yet is a snapshot of a state that may still change. Once the snapshot exists, the
+entries at or below its index can go: the state already includes them, and keeping them would mean
+the node holds the same information twice.
+
+Compacting does not move the log. A compacted log remembers where it starts, so an index still
+means the same thing on every node: the index is a position in the *cluster's* log, not in this
+node's memory of it. The next entry after a snapshot at 600 belongs at 601, and position 600 is the
+last one this node remembers having applied.
+
+## What a node that is too far behind does
+
+A follower that is behind the leader's log is normally caught up by entries: the leader sends what
+is missing. But a follower that is behind the *snapshot* cannot be caught up that way — the entries
+it is missing are exactly the ones the snapshot replaced. So the leader sends it the snapshot
+instead, and the follower adopts it: its log is dropped, its commit index moves to the snapshot's,
+and the next entry belongs right after it.
+
+The leader knows when this is the case: if the position it would send the peer to is at or before
+the snapshot's index, the peer needs the snapshot. That is one comparison, and it is the only thing
+that has to be true for a cluster to survive a restart of one of its nodes.
+
+## What the state is not
+
+This step is about *where* the snapshot is taken and *when* the log may be compacted. What the
+bytes inside it mean — and the rule that applying them must be deterministic, so that two nodes
+that applied the same entries hold the same state — is `5.10`'s subject. A snapshot that carried
+an unordered map's iteration order would break that rule, and no amount of care in this step would
+save it.
+
+## Where it is used
+
+The cluster drill (`5.15`) restarts a node that has been away for a while, and the consistency
+audit (`5.22`) checks that what the cluster committed is what every node holds — snapshots
+included. Neither step exists yet; this page is the shape they will use.
